he's dr. Alan sills the NFL's chief  medical officer and neurosurgeon and he  joins us on the program doc thanks for  joining us I was told you have surgery  in about 10 minutes  can they start without you or do you  need to be in there we're gonna have to  make a hard stop on this okay thanks for  having me on I explained what your job  is with the NFL so Dan I help oversee  all the health and safety efforts which  means working with all the club medical  staffs all of our medical committees  certainly our partners at the NFL  Players Association and their medical  staffs as well as all of our research  partners and engineers epidemiologists  and people that help us basically  touching and and helping to execute all  the things that affect health and safety  of players throughout the league when  you start to look at the NFL's plan  coming up this fall and I know it feels  like each week a lot happens you know if  we look back where we were three months  ago who knew we would be where we are  right now and where we're gonna be in  two or three months so is it your job to  sort of look down the road or do you  have to stay in the moment of trying to  adapt to whatever we're seeing on a  daily basis with this virus well I think  there's some both Dan and you made a  great point which I've been telling  people I say it feels like about five  years since we all started quarantine  Ian's sheltering in place it's actually  been probably about a hundred days and  that's the same time interval we have  from downs at the start of our season so  you think about how much we've learned  how much more awareness we have that the  new technologies that are emerging we  expect all that's going to continue in a  put us in a different place so I think  what we have to do is make the best  decisions we can at each stage with the  knowledge in hand but also try to  prepare and remain what the phrase I've  been saying to everybody flexible and  adaptable because in this situation  we're going to be adapting football to  the coronavirus not the virus to  football so it's crucially important  that we have that mindset of flexibility  and adaptability and using that new  knowledge along the way what's your  biggest concern this football season I  think like every one we're dealing with  a novel virus that no one can really  predict and so I think that it's  impossible to know which direction it's  going to take us and and and I think  that's true not just for the NFL but for  the country and for the world so I think  that's why we stay  very regular contact with public health  authorities with the CDC with the White  House Task Force with with public health  authorities in each state in each of our  club markets and we just have to work in  a very collective fashion as we do  what's best for not only just the NFL  but for the public at large are you 100%  confident we're playing football this  fall you know I don't think many of us  can be a hundred percent confident of  anything with regard to this virus I  would say that I'm very optimistic that  where we're tracking we're moving in  that direction but I think throughout  this damn we said look we have to be  cognizant of what's happening from a  public health standpoint the NFL's nine  is gonna put our head down and say we're  charging forward no matter what I think  you saw that with our draft right you  know there are a lot of people that said  well we couldn't or shouldn't do the  draft but we said look we've got this  set of things we'd like to do how can we  do that and do it in concert with what  the current public health  recommendations are and I think we wound  up with with a really unique and special  event that also raised a whole lot of  money for very needed relief effort so  that's the same approach we've got it  we've got to take here we've got to take  really careful look and work very  collaboratively again with our Players  Association their advisors with public  health authorities and make the best and  safest decision for all of our  stakeholders our players coaches fans  everybody that's in the NFL family what  do you think of the NBA plan you know  I've been a very regular contact with  the chief medical officer of the NBA and  all the professional sports leagues we  share with each other regularly our  learnings we're all confronting the  exact same issues and I think that  notionally we all have the same basic  approach to that there are obviously  some subtle nuances and differences from  one league to another just based on  operational issues but I think that  again the NBA is trying to do just what  we've done which is be very thoughtful  to really be cautious to work in a  stepwise fashion to keep everybody as  safe as possible and again we're all  learning from each other in this we talk  not only to each other in North America  but we talk to sports leagues around the  world the Bundesliga the Australian  Football League we're all trying to  share and collaborate and learn from  each other through this event and we  keep talking about well not me but you  know the experts keep talking about this  second wave and I said you know let me  know in the first wave ins because like  we don't we don't know this dock with  when does the first wave end and then  that second wave if it is coming back  so how do you prepare for that yeah I  think you're exactly right you know  there's a saying in the medical  community that all models are wrong but  some are actually useful and I think  that's true here you know that it's  really hard to predict what's going to  happen again I think we just have to be  flexible and adaptable we have to make  the best decisions we can with the  knowledge at hand and as you said that  that's very hard to predict and it's  also varies from region to region you  know what's happening in one part of the  country may be very different from the  other part of the country so I think as  I said we want to make sure we're hand  in glove with our players Association  and with public health authorities to  make the best decisions whatever the  situation on the ground may be at that  time you had a Zico Eliot some other  Cowboys some of the Texans who tested  positive if this was the week of a game  what would be protocol and with Zeke  Elliott and some of these other players  of you know what what safeguards would  be in place here and how long would they  be out so I think obviously I can't  comment specifically on any case but but  in general what our protocols are moving  towards is we want to identify anyone  who's newly infected at the earliest  possible moment and get them isolated  from the rest of the team environment  and again Dan this isn't just about  players this is coaches athletic  trainers strength and conditioning staff  anybody in what we're calling that team  ecosystem we want to identify them at  the earliest phase of infection remove  them from the rest of the environment  get them the appropriate treatment and  then watch and monitor that recovery so  our protocol will detail exactly what we  do at each step with that it's also  really important that we cleared them  appropriately to make sure they're not  infected when they come back in the  screen for things like some of the  cardiac complications that can happen  after kovat infection so we'll have all  that detailed out again we're working  hand in glove with the Players  Association on what that protocol looks  like what would be the minimum that  somebody would have to sit down if they  tested positive well again I think  that's that's based upon public health  guidelines and there's certain  guidelines depending on whether you have  symptoms when the last time your  symptoms were what your test status is  it's hard to give you an exact number  but I mean I think you're looking at  someone missing at least a couple weeks  time after a positive test it's not  going to be a test positive one day and  you're back two or three days later  there's going to be a time interval in  there again our knowledge when that may  continue to evolve a little bit but but  I'd say at a minimum  it's gonna be a couple weeks time what  will facemask look like this  this season will they be any alterations  to them well it's interesting you  mention that you've probably heard that  we've been working with with a group of  engineers to look at some alternative  designs of the face mask with sort of an  extended shield that covers parts of the  face mask I have to say when I first  heard about the idea I thought it was  probably the dumbest thing I heard I  didn't really think it would work but as  I looked at the designs and the  creativity I'm actually incredibly  impressed with that work and so again  we're working together with engineers  from the Players Association and  actually trying some of those models on  different players and getting feedback  and we'll see where that takes us but  I'm optimistic that we may be able to  find us something that's very useful  there again trying to help prevent  respiratory droplets from being spread  around while on the field I know we look  at these athletes and they're young  they're healthy but is a 300-pound  linemen healthy are they more at risk  than somebody who's a running back who  is you know weighs 225 yeah I think  that's something we're still trying to  learn there's still so much about this  virus that we don't know we do know  obviously again age and certain other  factors medical comorbidities that seem  to confirm or risk but how that  translates into an elite athlete  population we just don't know I think  the take-home message is we got to do  all we can to keep everybody safe  whether it's a 300-pound linemen or a  200-pound kicker quarterback we want to  try to mitigate risk for everyone in  there keep them as safe as possible and  as I said if they do become newly  infected get them isolated and treated  appropriately so that we don't spread  around to the rest of the team  go wash your hands good luck in surgery  haha thanks we always do that part and I  appreciate you having me on Thank You  doc that's dr. Alan sills the NFL chief  medical officer and oh by the way a  neurosurgeon

